+    // itself could round up (0.49999999999999994 gave 1). NaN gives 0; a value too large to have a
+    // fraction is already whole, and the narrowing cast saturates it (infinities too) as the Javadoc
+    // asks. Below that, a - floor(a) is exact. NaN is ruled out first because this compiler can get a
+    // comparison with NaN wrong (NaN < 0.5 comes out true).
     public static int round(float a) {
-        return (int) (a + signum(a) * 0.5f);
